.TH BZIP3 1 "2026-10-21" "bzip3-rs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bzip3 \- a block-sorting file compressor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bzip3
.RB [ \-e / \-z / \-d / \-t / \-r ]
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-j
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-1..\-14 ]
.RB [ \-\-fast ]
.RB [ \-\-best ]
.RB [ \-B ]
.RI [ files... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bzip3
compresses to and decompresses from the bzip3 format.
Every positional is an independent input; there is no
.I "in out"
form. With no files, standard input is processed to standard
output.
.B \-B
is accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.PP
Encode appends
.B .bz3
to the input name. Encode refuses inputs already ending in
.BR .bz3
.RI ( "already has .bz3 suffix" ,
exit 1).
Decode strips one trailing
.B .bz3
(case-sensitive); any other name decodes to
.IB name .out
with a
.I "Can't guess original name"
notice, suppressed by
.BR \-q .
.PP
A successful file-to-file run deletes the input unless
.B \-k
is given.
.BR \-c ,
.B \-t ,
.B \-r
and standard-input runs never delete; failures never delete.
The output inherits the input mode and modification time.
A failed run removes its partial output, except in recover mode.
.PP
.B \-\-rm
is rejected as an unknown flag.
.B \-f
only permits overwriting outputs; it never passes non-bzip3
bytes through.
.B bzip3
refuses to write compressed bytes to a terminal and refuses to
read compressed bytes from a terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-z ", " \-\-encode
Compress (default). The last mode flag wins.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-decode
Decompress.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-test
Verify compressed input without writing output.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recover
Decompress damaged input. Every block that the tool can find goes to the
output, so the offsets after a damaged block stay correct.
A block whose only fault is the checksum keeps its decoded bytes.
Any other damaged block gives zero bytes.
Each damaged block prints
.I "Writing invalid block"
with its number, its input offset, its fault and its output range
on standard error.
The line tells if the output bytes keep the decoded bytes or are zero.
A bad block header or missing data stops the run;
the blocks before it stay in the output.
The tool then prints
.I "Recovery stopped"
with the block number, the input offset and the output size.
Blocks have no sync marker, so the tool does not search for the next block.
A bad block size in the frame header prints
.I "Recovery mode: Proceeding."
and takes the block size from the block headers.
A bad signature or a short frame header is still fatal.
Recover mode never deletes the input file and keeps its output after a failure.
It differs from the reference: the reference exits 0 after a damaged block
and writes its decode buffer instead of zero bytes.
The reference also uses 511 MiB after a bad block size in the frame header.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-stdout
Write to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite an existing output file.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep
Keep (do not delete) input files on success.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress non-critical warnings only; errors always print.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print byte counts and ratios to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-b " " \fIN\fR ", " \-\-block=\fIN\fR
Block size: bare \fIN\fR or \fIN\fRM/\fIN\fRMiB is MiB, \fIN\fRK/\fIN\fRKiB
is KiB, case-insensitive, 65 KiB to 511 MiB. Default is 16M. The last of
.B \-b
and the level flags wins.
.TP
.BR \-1 .. \-14
Compression level: 1=65KiB, 2=128KiB, 3=256KiB, 4=512KiB, 5=1MiB,
6=2MiB, 7=4MiB, 8=8MiB, 9=16MiB (default), 10=32MiB, 11=64MiB,
12=128MiB, 13=256MiB, 14=511MiB. Levels are exact tokens;
clusters like \-19 are rejected. Encode only;
decode ignores levels.
.TP
.BR \-\-fast
Fastest mode, same as
.BR \-1 .
.TP
.BR \-\-best
Best mode, same as
.BR \-14 .
.TP
.BR \-j " " \fIN\fR ", " \-\-jobs=\fIN\fR
Worker thread count, 0 to 64. 0 (default) uses physical cores,
limited by available parallelism and clamped to 64. 1 runs serially;
2 to 64 select that many workers.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-batch
Accepted and ignored; every positional already runs alone.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, help, or version.
.TP
.B 1
Bad flags, unreadable or missing input, existing output without
.BR \-f ,
terminal refusal, or allocation failure. With several files the
worst per-file code is returned.
.TP
.B 2
Corrupt data: bad magic, truncated input, bad header size,
inconsistent headers, block or CRC failure, or a trailing byte.
Recover mode also returns 2 when it found a damaged block or stopped early.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bunzip3 (1),
.BR bz3cat (1),
.BR bz3grep (1),
.BR bz3diff (1),
.BR bz3more (1)
